<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<policyDefinitionResources xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema"
                           xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                           revision="1.0" schemaVersion="1.0"
                           xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/GroupPolicy/2006/07/PolicyDefinitions">
  <displayName>FeroSSH</displayName>
  <description>Machine policy for the FeroSSH SSH client.</description>
  <resources>
    <stringTable>
      <string id="FeroSSH">FeroSSH</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_FeroSSH_TeamPolicy">FeroSSH with team library policy support</string>
      <string id="SUPPORTED_FeroSSH_LogPolicy">FeroSSH with connection log policy support</string>

      <string id="TeamLibrary">Use this team library</string>
      <string id="TeamLibrary_Explain">Sets the team library every user of this PC uses: a .ferossh-library file exported from FeroSSH and signed with ssh-keygen -Y sign -n ferossh-library, with its .sig file beside it.

While this policy is enabled, the Team library path and signing key in each user's own FeroSSH settings are ignored, also for a portable FeroSSH (FeroSSH-data folder) and whatever settings file is used. Users see the library in Settings > Team library but cannot change or turn it off. The library's connections cannot be changed or replaced by a connection of the same name, and its security rules cannot be loosened.

Library file: the full path, for example \\server\share\team.ferossh-library. Environment variables are not expanded.

Signing keys: one OpenSSH public key line per entry (ssh-ed25519 AAAA... admin), as in the administrator's .pub file. The library must be signed by one of them.

Enabling this also makes FeroSSH connect nowhere while it cannot read a good library or its checked copy, unless "Require the team library to connect" is disabled.

This only binds users who are not administrators of the PC: an administrator can remove the policy. Use application control (AppLocker or WDAC) to stop users running another SSH client or an older FeroSSH.</string>

      <string id="RequireTeamLibrary">Require the team library to connect</string>
      <string id="RequireTeamLibrary_Explain">Applies while "Use this team library" is enabled.

Enabled or not configured: FeroSSH connects nowhere (SSH, SFTP, Telnet, WinRM, Remote Desktop) while it cannot read a team library with a good signature, and has no checked copy of one from an earlier read. Local shells and serial ports still open.

Disabled: without a good library FeroSSH connects as if there were none, with the user's own connections and rules.</string>

      <string id="OnlyTeamConnections">Allow only the team library's connections</string>
      <string id="OnlyTeamConnections_Explain">Applies while "Use this team library" is enabled.

Enabled: FeroSSH opens only the team library's connections, each exactly as the library defines it. Addresses typed at Connect to..., the user's own saved and imported connections, and their ~/.ssh/config are not used, and every jump host on the way must be a team connection too. Each user's own named credential (user, key and password) still applies to a team connection that names it. Without a good library FeroSSH connects nowhere.

Disabled or not configured: users can also open their own connections, under the team's security rules.</string>

      <string id="ConnectionLogDelete">Allow deleting connection log entries</string>
      <string id="ConnectionLogDelete_Explain">FeroSSH keeps a connection log on each PC: every connection made (SSH, SFTP, Telnet, WinRM, Remote Desktop), with when, to which address, through which jump hosts, with which host key, and how it ended (disconnected, lost, failed). Users see it in Tools > Connection log.

Disabled: nobody can delete entries from the connection log in FeroSSH. The Delete button is gone, and the page says the organisation keeps every entry.

Enabled: users can delete entries (after a question).

Not configured: deleting is up to the user, as when enabled.

This setting binds FeroSSH, not the file: a user can still delete the log file itself (%LOCALAPPDATA%\FeroSSH\connections.log, or connections.log.sealed while a master password vault is open). For a record users cannot remove, also enable "Copy the connection log to the Windows event log".</string>

      <string id="ConnectionLogEventLog">Copy the connection log to the Windows event log</string>
      <string id="ConnectionLogEventLog_Explain">Enabled: FeroSSH also writes every connection log entry to the Windows Application event log, source FeroSSH, with the user's account. Users who are not administrators cannot clear or change that log, and Windows Event Forwarding or a SIEM agent can collect it. A deletion from the connection log in FeroSSH is reported there too.

Event IDs: 1001 connected, 1002 disconnected, 1003 lost (reconnecting), 1004 failed, 1005 opened (Remote Desktop), 1010 entries deleted.

Register the event source once per PC, as an administrator, so Event Viewer shows the text without a warning: New-EventLog -LogName Application -Source FeroSSH

Disabled or not configured: the log is kept only in FeroSSH's own file.</string>
    </stringTable>
    <presentationTable>
      <presentation id="TeamLibrary">
        <textBox refId="TeamLibraryPath">
          <label>Library file:</label>
        </textBox>
        <listBox refId="TrustedKeys">Signing keys (one public key per entry):</listBox>
      </presentation>
    </presentationTable>
  </resources>
</policyDefinitionResources>
